From: SPNMX57 port
Subject: [PATCH] clk: qcom: actually build the IPQ CMN PLL driver

0063-v6.14-clk-qcom-Add-CMN-PLL-clock-controller-driver-for-IPQ-SoC.patch
adds drivers/clk/qcom/ipq-cmn-pll.c and nothing else. The upstream commit it
comes from also adds the Kconfig entry, the Makefile line and the shared
dt-bindings header; all three are missing here, so the symbol IPQ_CMN_PLL does
not exist, the file is never compiled, and it would not compile if it were.
target/linux/qualcommax/config-6.12 carries a stale
"# CONFIG_IPQ_CMN_PLL is not set" for a symbol Kconfig never defines, which is
what makes this easy to miss: it reads as a deliberate choice rather than a
dropped hunk.

The indices in the restored header follow the driver's own output-clock array
order, index 0 being the core PLL, matching the convention that
qcom,ipq5018-cmn-pll.h (added by 0065) already uses.

The consequence is not cosmetic on IPQ5018. The CMN PLL generates the ethernet
reference clocks handed to the external PHY package.
0067-v6.18-arm64-dts-ipq5018-Add-CMN-PLL-node.patch adds a cmn_pll node with
assigned-clock-rates-u64 = 9600000000, but with no driver bound to that node
nothing applies it, and the PLL is left in whatever state the bootloader
happened to leave it in.

On the Linksys SPNMX57 the vendor U-Boot only brings this PLL up inside a
branch gated on a /gmac_cfg node being present in its FDT, so it cannot be
relied on. The symptom there is very specific: the QCA8386 switch die answers
MDIO perfectly and latches every register write, because that path is clocked
by the host's own MDC, while the four QCA8084 EPHYs behind it never appear on
the bus at all, because their gp0..gp3_sys_clk domains have no reference clock
to run from.

--- a/drivers/clk/qcom/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/clk/qcom/Kconfig
@@ -190,6 +190,16 @@
 	  Say Y if you want to support CPU frequency scaling on
 	  ipq based devices.
 
+config IPQ_CMN_PLL
+	tristate "IPQ CMN PLL Clock Controller"
+	depends on ARM64 || COMPILE_TEST
+	help
+	  Support for CMN PLL clock controller on IPQ platform. The
+	  CMN PLL feeds the reference clocks to each ethernet device
+	  on the SoC.
+	  Say Y or M if you want to support CMN PLL clock on the IPQ
+	  based devices.
+
 config IPQ_GCC_4019
 	tristate "IPQ4019 Global Clock Controller"
 	help
--- a/drivers/clk/qcom/Makefile
+++ b/drivers/clk/qcom/Makefile
@@ -30,6 +30,7 @@
 obj-$(CONFIG_IPQ_APSS_PLL) += apss-ipq-pll.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_IPQ_APSS_6018) += apss-ipq6018.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_IPQ_GCC_4019) += gcc-ipq4019.o
+obj-$(CONFIG_IPQ_CMN_PLL) += ipq-cmn-pll.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_IPQ_GCC_5018) += gcc-ipq5018.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_IPQ_GCC_5332) += gcc-ipq5332.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_IPQ_GCC_6018) += gcc-ipq6018.o
--- a/include/dt-bindings/clock/qcom,ipq5424-cmn-pll.h
+++ b/include/dt-bindings/clock/qcom,ipq5424-cmn-pll.h
@@ -0,0 +1,22 @@
+/* SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0-only OR BSD-2-Clause) */
+/*
